Assembles two Neovim snippet artifacts (a Plover dictionary and a debug snippet table) from typed shorthand entries, distinguishing between non-terminal partials and terminal expansions to preserve Plover's backspace-correction behavior.

The critical distinction: non-terminal strokes (type-append intermediates) are never sentinel-wrapped; they're typed as plain literal text because Plover will backspace them and correct via its own logic. If the Nvim plugin had already rewritten that text (expanding a sentinel token into a snippet), Plover's backspace count would misalign and corrupt the buffer. Only terminal strokes (completed constructs, never extended further) are safe to sentinel-wrap and hand to Nvim. `insert_non_terminal` wraps values in `{^}...{^}` glue; `insert_terminal` wraps them via `wrap_inline_body` (which applies the sentinel marker) and also records the body for debug inspection.

`build_snippets(entries: &[TypedEntry]) -> Result<SnippetBuild, SnippetError>` renders each entry, classifies it as terminal or non-terminal, and inserts into the appropriate map. Collisions (same stroke mapping to two different values) are tracked and reported; no panic on duplicates, just flagged for later inspection. The Plover dictionary keys go in `plover_keys`; the snippet bodies (for inspection only) go in `snippets`.